  "version": 1,
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Leaks and Waste Pipes: Find the Hidden Rate",
    "description": "Recover a leak or waste-pipe rate from the difference between expected and actual filling.",
    "teachingApproach": "Treat a leak as an unseen outlet. First calculate what the known inlet system should do, then compare it with what actually happens. The gap between those rates is the leak. Progress from a single delayed fill to waste-pipe recovery, tank capacity, leaks beginning mid-process, and a late-discovered waste pipe.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 16, Pipes and Cisterns. Approved scope: Teach leaks as hidden outlets. Compare a pipe's normal fill rate with the slower observed net fill rate to obtain the leak rate. Cover finding leak-emptying time, finding a waste-pipe rate from two filling pipes and a combined time, tank capacity from a known litre-per-minute inlet plus leakage, and a leak that appears only after part of the tank is filled. Include late discovery of an open waste pipe. Keep signs meaningful: a negative contribution means water leaves the tank."
